Medical Center, Western Psychiatric Institute and Clinic","location":"PA 15213, United States"}},"id":"659b2c99b7154f5bb6e1a2e74063f2a4"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20040141770,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Buysse, D. J."},"organization":{}},"id":"4720e1dd4393478db1e8743fc6de600a"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20040141770,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Rose, L. R."},"organization":{}},"id":"f211884ce3d64ac79530fdcc8727c80a"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Reprint (Version printed in journal)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20040141770,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-22T03:18:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"STUDY OBJECTIVES: To determine whether wrist actigraphy is useful as a tool for space-based sleep research. Specifically, to determine whether bedtimes and waketimes can be identified from the actigraphic record, and whether actigraphic measures of sleep in space are related to polysomnographic (PSG) ones. DESIGN AND SETTING: Actigraphy, sleep diary, and Polysomnographic (PSG) measures of sleep were obtained from four subjects in two 72h measurement blocks occurring 2d and 12d into a 17d Space Shuttle mission in orbiting the earth in microgravity. PATIENTS: Four healthy male astronauts aged 38y - 47y. INTERVENTIONS: NA. MEASUREMENTS AND RESULTS: Sleep onset and offset at \"night\" could be quite clearly identified from the actigraphic record and were better estimated by actigraph than by diary. There was a high correlation between actigraphic and PSG estimates of sleep duration (r = 0.96) and sleep efficiency (r = 0.88), and a similarity in the mean estimates obtained. On a minute-by-minute basis, there was a good correlation between sleep stage and actigraphic movement counts, with a higher level of counts per minute recorded in epochs with lighter PSG sleep stages. There was also a high correlation (r = 0.90) between minutes of stage 0 (wake) occurring between bedtime and wake time, and number of non-zero actigraph epochs during the same interval. CONCLUSIONS: Actigraphy worked well in space both as a way of detecting bedtimes and waketimes, and as an indicant of sleep restlessness.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","publications":[{"volume":"22","submissionId":20040141770,"issue":"7","issn":"0161-8105","id":"432d6ec00c5149279165f65078a07804","publicationName":"Sleep","publicationDate":"1999-11-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":158.13646},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20040088714,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"62a1bbcf9f1e4f2a88634773a2a4ec82","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Life Sciences (General)"],"keywords":["Non-NASA Center","short duration","STS-90 Shuttle Project","manned","STS-95 Shuttle Project","NASA Discipline Regulatory Physiology","Flight Experiment","Photoperiod","Space Flight","Astronauts","Circadian Rhythm/physiology","Sleep/drug effects/physiology","Psychomotor Performance","Sleep, REM/physiology","Middle Aged","Lighting","Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S","Polysomnography","Hydrocortisone/urine","Human","Melatonin/pharmacology","Spacecraft","Time Factors","Adult","Male","Body Temperature","Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S","Activity Cycles","Female","Questionnaires"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20040088714,"ear":"NO","id":"31607b9cb48444a1bdd3382c1d870f73","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2011-08-23T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["ISSN: 0363-6119"],"title":"Sleep, performance, circadian rhythms, and light-dark cycles during two space shuttle flights","stiType":"REPRINT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-21T20:06:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Dijk, D. J."},"organization":{"name":"Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School","location":"Boston, Massachusetts 02115, United States"}},"id":"60a3edcca04e456e9061db778242146f"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Neri, D. F."},"organization":{}},"id":"3689b665a08743bfae3decaad71442a2"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Wyatt, J. K."},"organization":{}},"id":"b58579e886ad47469e7307039e5a10b6"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ronda, J. M."},"organization":{}},"id":"4d6b340f236848eda4ff73033e81fbce"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Riel, E."},"organization":{}},"id":"d46117499f5f448aadd53028a2ee0246"},{"sequence":5,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ritz-De Cecco, A."},"organization":{}},"id":"2df520aaf05643129752063a6e837f18"},{"sequence":6,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Hughes, R. J."},"organization":{}},"id":"ad350f6d99a144de91e8e4ff977e5a9b"},{"sequence":7,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Elliott, A. R."},"organization":{}},"id":"807c9ee623da4d31bee9a5761639589b"},{"sequence":8,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Prisk, G. K."},"organization":{}},"id":"6d99118bcdc44f8d949762312f7892cf"},{"sequence":9,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"West, J. B."},"organization":{}},"id":"482ebc72318a49a3984a38968046b7bf"},{"sequence":10,"submissionId":20040088714,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Czeisler, C. A."},"organization":{}},"id":"6a87ed084204417f860397c6b5d52368"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Reprint (Version printed in journal)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20040088714,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-21T20:06:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"CDMS","name":"Legacy CDMS","id":"092d6e0881874968859b972d39a888dc"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Sleep, circadian rhythm, and neurobehavioral performance measures were obtained in five astronauts before, during, and after 16-day or 10-day space missions. In space, scheduled rest-activity cycles were 20-35 min shorter than 24 h. Light-dark cycles were highly variable on the flight deck, and daytime illuminances in other compartments of the spacecraft were very low (5.0-79.4 lx). In space, the amplitude of the body temperature rhythm was reduced and the circadian rhythm of urinary cortisol appeared misaligned relative to the imposed non-24-h sleep-wake schedule. Neurobehavioral performance decrements were observed. Sleep duration, assessed by questionnaires and actigraphy, was only approximately 6.5 h/day. Subjective sleep quality diminished. Polysomnography revealed more wakefulness and less slow-wave sleep during the final third of sleep episodes. Administration of melatonin (0.3 mg) on alternate nights did not improve sleep. After return to earth, rapid eye movement (REM) sleep was markedly increased. Crewmembers on these flights experienced circadian rhythm disturbances, sleep loss, decrements in neurobehavioral performance, and postflight changes in REM sleep.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","publications":[{"volume":"281","submissionId":20040088714,"issue":"5","issn":"0363-6119","id":"26d7be9b1af24970aa54202eb6a15cd7","publicationName":"American journal of physiology. Regulatory, integrative and comparative physiology","publicationDate":"2001-11-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":156.28473},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20140016946,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"a2312b5808bb484c899d529d0f569ea5","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Behavioral Sciences","Aerospace Medicine"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20140016946,"ear":"NO","id":"dcb4117fd763476b98d06e06a81ad566","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-17T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["JSC-CN-32255","Report Number: JSC-CN-32255"],"title":"Fatigue in U.S. Astronauts Onboard the International Space Station: Environmental Factors, Operational Impacts, and Implementation of Countermeasures","stiType":"ABSTRACT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2014-12-03T15:36:08.2100000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20140016946,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Scheuring, R. A."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"2837048115234f4eb3c242cf3ab1bd4d"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20140016946,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Moomaw, R. C."},"organization":{"name":"Wyle Labs., Inc.","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"a3e528953b2f402fb9cc6a8cbf9a9f15"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20140016946,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Johnston, S. L."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"386bd8f427ae452ca947cc5261d6ea7d"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Abstract","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20140016946,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2014-12-03T15:36:08.2100000+00:00","center":{"code":"JSC","name":"Johnson Space Center","id":"dd46994b91a94cb2a2722617cbbadc55"},"onlyAbstract":true,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Since 2000, US astronauts have been supporting missions up to a six month duration on the International Space Station (ISS). Crewmembers have experienced fatigue for reasons similar to military deployments. Astronauts experience psychological stressors such as heavy workloads, extended duty periods, circadian misalignment, inadequate/ineffective sleep, and loss of the environmental cues of a gravity environment. Complicating the psychological stressors are environmental factors; distracting background noise, unexpected and variable mission schedules, unfavorable thermal control, elevated CO2 levels, and an unusual sleep environment with schedules that impinge on pre‐sleep periods. Physiological contributors to poor sleep and fatigue include a cephalad fluid shift and back pain. Restful sleep is further challenged due to a lack of gravity‐related proprioceptive cues and need for restraints. The term \"space fog\" has been used by astronauts to describe a phenomenon of forgetfulness, slowed reaction time and transient confusion while trying to complete tasks. There is a distinct temporal correlation with arrival on the Space Station and the onset of slowed cognitive skills and a spontaneous resolution that may take up to 6 weeks. The Genesis of this phenomenon may be chronic fatigue secondary to transitioning from a planar environment to a 360deg microgravity perspective. Recently, countermeasures to improve sleep duration and quality in astronauts on the ISS have been instituted with moderate degrees of success as measured by self‐reaction time (psychomotor vigilance task testing), actigraphy, and subjective reports. Judicious use of stimulants and hypnotics, light therapy, controlled sleep periods and sleep shifting and reducing ambient CO2 levels are a few of the most promising countermeasures being used in space to improve sleep and reduce fatigue.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"submissionId":20140016946,"name":"Aerospace Medical Association","location":"Alexandria, VA, United States","id":"6113bfaac47649f3885d80592e40e237"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20140016946,"endDate":"2015-05-14T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"Aerospace Medical Association","location":"Alexandria, VA, United States"}},"meetingId":"f60100418d01496d9d05db31d22cea3c","id":"ffe173eb81b04783942056ad00f207e7"}],"name":"Aerospace Medical Association Annual Scientific Meeting","location":"Lake Buena Vista, FL","id":"f60100418d01496d9d05db31d22cea3c","startDate":"2015-05-10T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20140016946,"id":"288e2165108d4fbc9a68183d7d45cd1c","publicationDate":"2014-01-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20140016946.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20140016946/downloads/20140016946.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20140016946/downloads/20140016946.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20140016946/downloads/20140016946.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"}],"aggregations":{"created":{"buckets":[{"key_as_string":"2013","key":1356998400000,"doc_count":2},{"key_as_string":"2014","key":1388534400000,"doc_count":1}]},"author":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"Buysse, D. J.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Czeisler, C. A.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Dijk, D. J.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Elliott, A. R.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Hughes, R. J.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Johnston, S. L.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Monk, T. H.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Moomaw, R. C.","doc_count":1},{"key":"Neri, D. F.","doc_coun